# eOn SVN r2549, Fortran enabled

The client behind the `eon-svn` rows is SVN revision 2549 of
<https://theory.cm.utexas.edu/svn/eon>. `eonclient -v` prints
`eonclient version r2549`. This build does not pass `NO_FORTRAN`.
The seven Fortran potentials are compiled in.

A directory `svn export -r 2549` of the whole project stops on
historical paths the server forbids. The client tree was fetched
file by file: `svn list -r 2549` of each directory, then
`svn export --force -r 2549` of each file, from
`https://theory.cm.utexas.edu/svn/eon/client`. That fetch wrote 679
files and skipped none. QSC is not a directory at r2549. The link
drops it. The sources are not in this repository. The binary used
for the rows below was installed at `$HOME/opt/eon-svn/bin/eonclient`
and selected with `EON_SVN_CLIENT`.

## Compilers

Recorded at the build, 2026-10-18T10:59:55Z:

```
gcc (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0
g++ (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0
GNU Fortran (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0
gfortran -dumpversion: 13
g++ -dumpmachine: x86_64-linux-gnu
GNU Make 4.3
svn, version 1.14.3 (r1914484)
```

The C++ client is the r2549 `Makefile`, not CMake. `libcurl` is the
link dependency from that makefile (`-lcurl`). BLAS and LAPACK were
present on the machine and are not on the link line.

## Edits the r2549 tree needs before `make`

`client/Rules.mk` lists `potentials/QSC`. That directory is not in
r2549, so these three lines were removed:

```
POTDIRS += ./potentials/QSC/
LIBS += ./potentials/QSC/libQSC.a
POTENTIALS += "+QSC"
```

`client/Potential.cpp` includes `potentials/QSC/QSC.h` and constructs
`QSC()` outside the `NO_FORTRAN` guard. Both were removed. The
`POT_QSC` string stays. A request for `qsc` falls through to
`Unknown Potential`.

`client/Makefile` links gfortran with a Homebrew path that is not on
this machine:

```
LDFLAGS += -L/usr/local/Cellar/gcc/9.2.0_3/lib/gcc/9 -lgfortran
```

That line was changed to the gfortran 13 library directory printed by
`gfortran -print-file-name=libgfortran.so`:

```
LDFLAGS += -L/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-linux-gnu/13 -lgfortran
```

`client/version.sh` runs `svnversion`. An export is not a working
copy, so `svnversion` does not print `2549`. The script now keeps a
plain integer from `svnversion` and otherwise writes `2549`, which is
the revision that was exported. The linked client prints
`eonclient version r2549`.

## Build command

`NO_FORTRAN` was unset. From `client/`:

```
make -j8
```

Flags the makefile actually passed:

```
CC=gcc
CXX=g++
FC=gfortran
OPT=-O2
CXXFLAGS=-Wall -Wno-unused-local-typedefs -Wno-unused-function -Wfatal-errors -O2 -DLINUX -DNDEBUG
DEPFLAGS=-MMD
FFLAGS=-O2 -ffpe-trap=invalid,zero,overflow
LDFLAGS=-lcurl -ldl -L/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-linux-gnu/13 -lgfortran
AR=ar cru
```

Fortran objects were compiled as
`gfortran -O2 -ffpe-trap=invalid,zero,overflow -c`. The link line
includes `libAL.a`, `libLenosky.a`, `libSW.a`, `libTersoff.a`,
`libEDIP.a`, `libtip4p_h.a`, and `libFeHe.a`. The makefile banner was:

```
DEBUG: No
POTENTIALS: +EMT +MORSE +LJ +LJ +EAM +H2O +H2O_Pt +IMD +EXT_POT +Aluminum +Lenosky +SW +Tersoff +EDIP +H2O_H +FeHe -PyAMFF -LAMMPS -ASE -NEW_POT +VASP -MPI_POT
```

`ldd` on the installed binary resolves `libgfortran.so.5`. The
version lines were:

```
eonclient version r2549
          compiled Sun Oct 18 10:59:57 AM UTC 2026
```

## Fortran potentials the client accepts

`#ifndef NO_FORTRAN` in `Potential.cpp` registers these names:
`eam_al`, `lenosky_si`, `sw_si`, `tersoff_si`, `edip`, `tip4p_h`,
`fehe`. They are the Aluminum, Lenosky, SW, Tersoff, EDIP, H2O_H, and
FeHe libraries in the banner.

`eonclient -s -p NAME pair.con` was run on this two-atom con. The box
is a 10 Angstrom cube. Both atoms are Al, 2.8 Angstrom apart. Every
Fortran name returned exit 0 and an energy. `not_a_potential` printed
`Unknown Potential: not_a_potential` and exited 1.

```
eam_al        -1.3914401531
lenosky_si    -1.7356583379
sw_si         -1.5309509672
tersoff_si    -1.4413938263
edip          -1.3837200533
tip4p_h        0.0000000000
fehe          -0.0007742742
```

The Si and water names were given an aluminum pair, so those energies
are only a probe that the symbol is linked. `eam_al` is the Aluminum
EAM potential.

## hTST/Al

No hTST/Al row is scored from this client. `problems/hTST/Al.toml`
requires `barrier_force_calls`, `barrier_jobs`, `rate_force_calls`,
and `rate_jobs`. The harness reducers score `force_calls` and
`success` with PAR-2 (`reducer = harness`) or an AKMC connected-saddle
count. None of them reads an AKMC `processtable` and
`search_results.txt` and emits those four fields. The 2014 reduction
that does is `archive/entries/hTST/Al/eon-dimer-cg/report.py`: the
mean, over repetitions, of the searches and force calls until every
reference barrier below 0.4 eV has been found, and until the summed
rate reaches 0.999 of the reference rate. That reducer is not in the
harness. The problem's `[scoring.v2]` block lists the PAR-2 columns
and does not say which of the four hTST costs is the trial cost, what
the cap is, or what a failed repetition is. A PAR-2 number for hTST/Al
would be invented, so it is not written.

## Pooled rows

Each harness file under `results/campaign/eon-svn-r2549-` for the
templates rerun on this client averages two replicates of the same
start. The first replicate is the run already on `entries/eon-svn-all`
at `1f8246a` (`svn-all`, the `NO_FORTRAN` client). The second is the
rerun with this binary (`fortran`), `EON_SVN_CLIENT` pointing at the
client above. When the files were pooled, `origin/entries/eon-svn-all`
was `1a86921`, then `5a6f734`. Those tips merge main onto `1f8246a`
and do not change the `eon-svn-r2549` campaign files, so there is no
third replicate.

A start id is the campaign `(seed, start)` pair. For each id the
stored `force_calls` is the mean of the replicate counts. `n` is the
number of distinct starts. `n_replicates` is 2 on every row. Success
is the shared boolean when the replicates agree. If they disagreed,
the start would record both outcomes and `success` would be the
fraction of replicates that succeeded. None disagreed. PAR-2, the
call columns, `nfailed`, and the Wilson interval are `score_v2` on
those per-start means. The dat file also has `n_differing`, the
number of starts whose force-call counts differ.

One lowest-eigenvector Lanczos start (`0083`) hit
`TimeoutError: timed out` under the eight-worker pool, with zero
force calls. That start was run again by itself and the timeout row
was replaced before the average.

### Reproducibility, svn-all versus Fortran

`identical` counts starts whose force-call counts and success bits
match. `differing` counts the rest. `max |Δ|` is the largest absolute
force-call difference. Every template has 2 replicates on every start.

| template | n | identical | differing | max \|Δ\| |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| eon-svn/ts-lj38-dimer | 200 | 200 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/ts-lj38-lanczos | 200 | 199 | 1 | 55 |
| eon-svn/ts-pt-dimer | 49 | 49 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/ts-pt-lanczos | 49 | 49 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/de-lj38-ci | 50 | 50 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/de-lj38-neb | 50 | 50 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/de-pt-ci | 59 | 59 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/de-pt-neb | 59 | 59 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/de-2ts-ci | 51 | 51 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/de-2ts-neb | 51 | 51 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/neb-pt-ci | 13 | 13 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/neb-pt-neb | 13 | 13 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/low-lj38-dimer | 200 | 200 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/low-lj38-lanczos | 200 | 200 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/lj38-noise-0 | 1000 | 1000 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/lj38-noise-1e-4 | 1000 | 1000 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/lj38-noise-1e-3 | 1000 | 1000 | 0 | 0 |
| eon-svn/lj38-noise-1e-2 | 1000 | 1000 | 0 | 0 |

The one differing start is `eon-svn/ts-lj38-lanczos`, seed `1`, start
`0167`. Both replicates succeeded. `svn-all` used 265 force calls
(`client_calls` 266). `fortran` used 210 force calls (`client_calls`
210). The stored count is the mean, 237.5. The separate `score_v2`
PAR-2 values are `svn-all` 2974.825 and `fortran` 2974.55. The
per-start mean scores to 2974.6875.

Fourteen cap failures match on `force_calls` (2500), `success`
(false), and failure class (`cap`), and differ only in sidecar
fields. The `svn-all` rows store `client_calls` 40007, `client_reason`
`2`, and `extrema`. The `fortran` rows store an `error` string
(`ForceCallCap`) and omit those fields. They are identical starts in
the table above. The ci starts are `pair_02`, `pair_17`, `pair_18`,
`pair_28`, and `pair_38`. The neb starts are `pair_05`, `pair_09`,
`pair_13`, `pair_17`, `pair_18`, `pair_28`, `pair_37`, `pair_38`, and
`pair_42`.

## hTST windows

`optbench.htst` reduces `states/0/processtable` and
`search_results.txt` the way the 2014 `report.py` scripts do. A start
is one barrier window. The client is this r2549 binary.
`eon-svn/htst-al-dimer` calls `eam_al`. `eon-svn/htst-pt-dimer` calls
`morse_pt`. Both stayed on state 0, missed no reference barriers, and
are not partial. The aluminium run resumed one state after the r2549
communicator stopped submitting jobs. `seconds` on that dat is the
completing process, 3102.16. The archived rows are the 2014 means.

| row | barrier jobs | barrier force calls | barrier windows | rate jobs | rate force calls | rate windows |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| archive Al eon-dimer-cg | 48 | 2.887e+04 | 639 | 1511 | 8.979e+05 | 20 |
| r2549 Al eon-svn/htst-al-dimer | 50 | 1.966e+04 | 639 | 1600 | 6.206e+05 | 19 |
| archive Al eon-dimer-cg2 | 46 | 2.107e+04 | 432 | 1671 | 7.663e+05 | 11 |
| archive Pt eon-dimer-cg | 1050 | 4.960e+05 | 13 | 54 | 2.563e+04 | 276 |
| r2549 Pt eon-svn/htst-pt-dimer | 1062 | 5.060e+05 | 13 | 57 | 2.716e+04 | 242 |

The r2549 aluminium search log has 32346 rows. The platinum log has
13824. The archive aluminium dimer-cg log that the reducer test reads
has 30997.
